# Cryptography Techniques — Hashing, File Encryption & BitLocker

**Course:** SEC311 Ethical Hacking
**Alignment:** CEH v13 — Cryptography
**Environment:** Isolated training lab using Windows Server (ACIDC01) and Windows 11 (ACIWIN11)

> **Lab Safety Notice:** This activity was completed inside an isolated and authorized training environment. The techniques documented here are intended for education and defensive understanding only.

---

## Overview

This lab was the defensive counterpart to the rest of the course. Instead of breaking into systems, I was implementing the controls that protect them — using cryptography to enforce two legs of the CIA triad: **integrity** (through hashing) and **confidentiality** (through file and full-disk encryption).

The main objective was not just to run the tools, but to understand *when to use which control* — hashing vs. file encryption vs. full-disk encryption — and how each one maps to a different security goal.

---

## The Three Controls and What Each One Does

A key part of this lab was understanding that these are three different jobs, not interchangeable tools:

| Control | Security Goal | What It Actually Does |
| ------- | ------------- | --------------------- |
| **Hashing** | Integrity | Creates a one-way fingerprint of a file to prove it hasn't been altered. Cannot be reversed to recover the data. |
| **File Encryption** | Confidentiality (per file) | Scrambles individual files so only someone with the key can read them. |
| **Full-Disk Encryption** | Confidentiality (at rest) | Encrypts an entire drive so a stolen disk or laptop is unreadable without the key. |

The clearest way I found to frame it: **hashing proves data wasn't changed; encryption keeps data from being read.** Two different problems, two different tools.

